PROTOTYPE, not for sending. Plain diff against systemd main 6bd4689 (263~devel), made 2026-11-15.

sd-journal: take the next entry from a priority queue of archived files

real_journal_next() asks every open file for its next entry and compares
them all, for every entry: O(F) per entry, so reading N entries in F files
costs O(N x F), and F grows with the journal as rotation adds files.

After a linear walk, keep the archived files that hold a candidate entry
in a Prioq, ordered by compare_locations() (the order the linear walk uses),
ties by the file's position in the linear walk (the linear walk keeps the
first of equal candidates). The next call then
 - advances the file the last entry came from and queues it again,
 - walks the files that are not archived (they may grow) linearly, as before,
 - drops the queue's top candidates that are copies of the entry just
   returned (what the linear walk does for every file),
 - takes the smaller of the queue's top and the best unarchived candidate.
That is O(log F) per entry (plus the unarchived files, normally one or
two). The queue is dropped by any seek, any change of matches and any added
or removed file (sd_journal.files_generation), and the next call walks
linearly and builds it again. Below 16 files it is not built.

Not covered, and why this is a prototype:
 - compare_locations() is not a strict weak order (it mixes sequence numbers
   with time, calls entries of two machines equal). A heap and a linear scan
   can disagree when it is not transitive. Output was identical on every set
   tried, including 12 boots with overlapping time windows, but that is
   evidence, not a proof; one machine ID only was tested.
 - Archived files are assumed not to change while open, as ARCHIVED implies.
 - No test with a writer running (follow mode) was made.
 - No test was added to systemd's test suite.

